% ============================================================================
% start-codon — which mRNA codons NCBI's Standard Genetic Code table marks as
% capable of initiating translation (adj-facts-stdlib, BIOLOGY).
% ============================================================================
% A sibling to the already-shipped `genetic-code.adj` (which recalls
% `codon_amino_acid(codon, amino_acid)` for all 64 codons): a new
% `start_codon(codon, role)` table names WHICH codons the source's own
% "Starts" annotation line flags, information the existing table's schema has
% no room for. `genetic-code.adj`'s own `source` field already quotes, in
% full, NCBI's five-line translation-table block VERBATIM — including a
% `Starts` line the shipped table only ever surfaces as a single inline `%`
% comment on the `atg` row ("the START codon"), leaving the fact
% un-queryable and leaving the OTHER two codons the same line flags entirely
% unrecorded. Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? start_codon(atg, $Role)   % start
%     ? start_codon(gcc, $Role)   % abstain — not flagged by the source
%
% A native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5, a TWO-column table like
% `genetic-code.adj`'s own `codon_amino_acid`): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `start_codon(codon, role)` carrying the citation, so the lookup
% above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns the role WITH
% its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and abstains on any codon
% the source's `Starts` line does not mark.
%
% DECODING THE SOURCE (no new WebFetch — the artifact is already quoted
% verbatim inside `genetic-code.adj`'s own `source` field, and in this
% file's own `source` below. THE BLOCK DRAWN HERE IS RE-INDENTED FOR
% READING and is NOT the page's own bytes: it pads `AAs` on the wrong side
% so all five labels line up, where the page indents line 1 by four spaces
% and puts two spaces after `AAs`. Its data columns are internally aligned,
% so the decoding below is sound — but for the page's bytes read `source`,
% not this drawing.)
% ------------------------------------------------------------------------
% NCBI's canonical 5-line block aligns four lines column-by-column; column i
% names one codon (`Base1[i] Base2[i] Base3[i]`) and, when the `Starts` line
% carries an `M` at that column, flags that codon as a start site:
%
%   AAs    = FFLLSSSSYY**CC*WLLLLPPPPHHQQRRRRIIIMTTTTNNKKSSRRVVVVAAAADDEEGGGG
%   Starts = ---M------**--*----M---------------M----------------------------
%   Base1  = TTTTTTTTTTTTTTTTCCCCCCCCCCCCCCCCAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAGGGGGGGGGGGGGGGG
%   Base2  = TTTTCCCCAAAAGGGGTTTTCCCCAAAAGGGGTTTTCCCCAAAAGGGGTTTTCCCCAAAAGGGG
%   Base3  = TCAGTCAGTCAGTCAGTCAGTCAGTCAGTCAGTCAGTCAGTCAGTCAGTCAGTCAGTCAGTCAG
%
% The `Starts` line carries exactly three `M`s, at columns 4, 20, and 36.
% Reading Base1/Base2/Base3 at those same three columns gives the three
% flagged codons:
%
%     column   Base1  Base2  Base3   codon   Starts mark
%     ------   -----  -----  -----   -----   -----------
%     4        T      T      G       ttg     M
%     20       C      T      G       ctg     M
%     36       A      T      G       atg     M
%
% This table deliberately does NOT pair these codons with an amino acid —
% translation always initiates with the same initiator methionine regardless
% of which start codon recruited it, so a `(codon, amino_acid)` pairing for
% `ttg`/`ctg` would misstate biology by implying those codons install
% leucine at the start position. `role` instead names only what the source
% itself asserts: that NCBI's own annotation marks this codon as capable of
% initiating translation, nothing more.
%
% Provenance: reproduces, byte-for-byte, the SAME NCBI "Genetic Codes" page
% (translation table 1, "The Standard Code" / SGC0) already cited by
% `genetic-code.adj` — no new source, only a different schema decoding a
% line of the SAME already-quoted artifact. `trust authoritative` —
% ncbi.nlm.nih.gov is NIH/NLM, a primary source, the same tier
% `genetic-code.adj` already carries. (See ../README.md;
% feedback_nothing_human_authored, feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts,
% feedback_no_byte_arithmetic_for_llm.)
%
% INSTALLMENT 4h RESTORED THE PAGE'S INDENTATION, and until it did, the
% claim above was false by TWELVE characters. NCBI serves this block inside
% a `<pre>` that indents LINE 1 BY FOUR SPACES and every later line by two,
% so all five data columns land at offset 11, and the shipped value had
% every one of those twelve stripped. What broke verbatimness was the EIGHT
% interior spaces on lines 2-5; the four on line 1 could not, which is why
% the first attempt at this repair — restoring only eight — produced a value
% that WAS still a byte-exact, unique substring of the page and was
% nevertheless wrong: its span began four characters into line 1, so the
% excerpt no longer stood on its own columns, and it was MORE misaligned
% than the value it replaced. Security review caught that by decoding it,
% not by matching it. This library reads the `Starts` line, and
% Starts/Base1/Base2/Base3 stayed mutually aligned in every variant — only
% `AAs` was ever displaced — so THIS table's three rows always decoded
% correctly, before and after. The `atg`-decodes-to-T damage belongs to
% `genetic-code.adj`, which reads the `AAs` line; an earlier draft of this
% note copied that sentence over and claimed damage to a decode path this
% file's own "This table deliberately does NOT pair these codons with an
% amino acid" paragraph says it never uses.
%
% SO THE CHECK THAT MATTERS IS NOT "is it a substring". Stripping leading
% whitespace from a span's first line always leaves one, so a substring test
% passes the defective value; an earlier draft of this note credited exactly
% that test, which could not have caught what it was cited for.
%
% THREE properties separate the shipped value from the first attempt, and
% measurement rather than argument settles which: the match BEGINS AT A LINE
% BOUNDARY, all five data columns land at ONE offset, and `atg` DECODES to M
% on the `AAs` line. The first attempt failed all three while still passing
% substring and uniqueness — which is why those two are asserted for their
% own sake and credited with separating nothing. Two rounds got this wrong
% in opposite directions: one credited uniqueness with power it lacks, the
% next denied power to the column and decode arms that have it.
% `tools/verify_ncbi_pre_span.py` in this package now runs the whole battery
% on a reconstructed first attempt and asserts exactly which arms must fail,
% so this paragraph reports a test that runs rather than standing beside
% one. Installment 4g's recipe already had the anchoring — it wrote the
% matched slice itself — and 4h's substring restatement lost it. CI pins the
% field, and mutants that strip the interior indent OR line 1's anchor both
% redden the test.
%
% Restoring it decided none of the questions held on #14111. Whitespace
% inside a `<pre>` is RENDERED whitespace — the browser renders all twelve
% of them, four on line 1 and two on each line after — so carrying them is
% the same call as carrying a page's U+00A0. That is NOT the case filed on
% #14111 the same day, where newlines turn up inside an ordinary wrapped
% paragraph: those are HTML source formatting that renders as a single
% space, and which reading of `source` governs them is the owner's call.
% The two look identical in a diff and are opposite in kind, which is why
% each candidate gets its page read.
%
% Worth noting how long it went unseen: no instrument in this effort
% compares a value to RAW HTML. The contiguity sweep compares against the
% extractor's output, which normalises whitespace, and the CI pins compare
% the engine's output to this field — the field to itself.
% ============================================================================

table start_codon {
    columns codon, role

    row (ttg, start)
    row (ctg, start)
    row (atg, start)

    source "    AAs  = FFLLSSSSYY**CC*WLLLLPPPPHHQQRRRRIIIMTTTTNNKKSSRRVVVVAAAADDEEGGGG\n  Starts = ---M------**--*----M---------------M----------------------------\n  Base1  = TTTTTTTTTTTTTTTTCCCCCCCCCCCCCCCCAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAGGGGGGGGGGGGGGGG\n  Base2  = TTTTCCCCAAAAGGGGTTTTCCCCAAAAGGGGTTTTCCCCAAAAGGGGTTTTCCCCAAAAGGGG\n  Base3  = TCAGTCAGTCAGTCAGTCAGTCAGTCAGTCAGTCAGTCAGTCAGTCAGTCAGTCAGTCAGTCAG"
    locator "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/Taxonomy/Utils/wprintgc.cgi"
    trust authoritative
}
